p.src:
The same reasoning applies to light escaping from the Sun. Its spectral lines arrive redshifted relative to identical lines measured in the laboratory. For a spherical source of mass $M$ and radius $R$, the weak field scale is $z
  \equiv
  \frac{\nu_{\mathrm{emit}}-\nu_{\mathrm{receive}}}
       {\nu_{\mathrm{receive}}}
  \simeq
  \frac{GM}{Rc^{2}}
  =
  \frac{v_{\mathrm{esc}}^{2}}{2c^{2}}.$ Large radius alone is not decisive. What matters is compactness, or equivalently the escape speed. White dwarfs and neutron stars produce larger shifts than ordinary stars, and the redshift becomes extreme near a black-hole horizon.

