p.src:
If $A^{m}$ and $B^{n}$ are contravariant vectors, their component products provide a rank-two example, $T^{mn}=A^{m}B^{n}.$ There are $d^{2}$ components, and each upper index brings its own direct Jacobian: $\boxed{
  T_{(y)}^{pq}
  =
  \frac{\partial y^{p}}{\partial x^{m}}
  \frac{\partial y^{q}}{\partial x^{n}}
  T_{(x)}^{mn}.}$ Not every rank-two tensor need be a single product of two vectors; the product is a convenient way to discover the transformation pattern.

