p.src:
After the break, begin again with the most familiar geometry: Euclidean space in Cartesian coordinates. For an infinitesimal displacement, Pythagoras gives $ds^{2}
  =
  (dx^{1})^{2}+(dx^{2})^{2}+\cdots+(dx^{d})^{2}.$ The superscript on $x^{m}$ labels a component; the superscript on $ds^{2}$ is an actual square. Introduce the Kronecker delta, $\delta_{mn}
  =
  \begin{cases}
    1, & m=n,\\
    0, & m\ne n,
  \end{cases}$ and write $\boxed{
  ds^{2}
  =
  \delta_{mn}\,dx^{m}dx^{n}.}$

