p.src:
Finally take a unit sphere. Let $\phi$ be the polar angle measured from the north pole and let $\theta$ be the azimuthal angle. A displacement in the $\phi$ direction has length $d\phi$. At fixed $\phi$, the circle of latitude has radius $\sin\phi$, so an azimuthal displacement has length $\sin\phi\,d\theta$. The two coordinate directions are orthogonal, and $\boxed{
  ds^{2}
  =
  d\phi^{2}
  +
  \sin^{2}\phi\,d\theta^{2}.}$ For a sphere of radius $R$, the right-hand side is multiplied by $R^{2}$.

